    <name>
      <text>Integration by substitution</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>Find  \( \displaystyle \int {@f_t@}\, dt\)  by use of substitution method.<br></p><p>[[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]][[feedback:prt1]]</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p><strong>Worked solution<br></strong></p>
<p>Let us solve \( \displaystyle \int {@f_t@}\, dt\) by substitution method.<br>
    Let \(u={@constant_denom_1@}-{@coeff_denom_2@}t\)<br>
    Differentiating \(u\) with respect to \(t\) we have;<br>
    \(\frac{du}{dt}=-{@coeff_denom_2@}\), making \(dt\) the subject we have that \(dt=\frac{du}{-{@coeff_denom_2@}}\)<br>
    Replacing the obtained expressions \(u\) and \(dt\) back to the given integral we have;<br>
    \( \displaystyle \int {@f_t@}\, dt = \displaystyle \int {@g_t@}\, du=&nbsp;{@tas@} \).</p>
<p>Then,</p>
<p>\( \displaystyle \int {@f_t@}\, dt={@tas@}\)</p>
<p>But \(u={@constant_denom_1@}-{@coeff_denom_2@}t\)</p>
<p>Therefore,</p>
<p>\( \displaystyle \int {@f_t@}\, dt\)={@ta_1@}, where \(c\) is the constant of integration. </p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
    <defaultgrade>1</defaultgrade>
    <penalty>0.1</penalty>
    <hidden>0</hidden>
    <idnumber/>
    <stackversion>
      <text>2023060500</text>
